---
<Layout
  title="The Authorial Capacity Constraint"
  description="Working Paper 001: epistemic stewardship, defensibility, separable contribution and human responsibility under machine-amplified cognitive abundance."
>
  <header class="page-head paper-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working Paper 001 · v0.2 · 7 September 2026</p>
      <h1>The Authorial Capacity Constraint</h1>
      <p class="lede">Epistemic stewardship under cognitive abundance.</p>
      <div class="paper-meta" aria-label="Publication status">
        <span>Exploratory</span>
        <span>Machine-assisted</span>
        <span>Not peer reviewed</span>
        <span>Open for criticism</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Epistemic status</p>
        <h2>This paper begins with a limitation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The sophistication of this text may exceed the present ability of its human curator to defend every argument it contains.</p>
        <p>This is not presented as a disclaimer that dissolves responsibility. It is the problem under study. Large language models can increase the quality, range and apparent authority of an intellectual artefact faster than they increase the comprehension of the person directing and publishing it.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">Accordingly, this version should be read as an exploratory research object: a proposal to be challenged, refined, independently checked and, where possible, converted into testable claims. Publication establishes provenance and invites criticism; it does not certify mastery.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="paper-body">
    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">Abstract</p>
        <p class="paper-abstract">Machine cognition can now produce arguments, code, analyses and other intellectual artefacts whose sophistication exceeds the unaided competence of the human who prompted, selected or published them. This decouples artefact quality from authorial comprehension, weakening an assumption embedded in traditional authorship: that the person presenting a claim can also explain, interrogate and defend it. This paper names that problem the <strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint</strong>. It proposes the <strong>Principle of Defensible Stewardship</strong>: where cognitive production is delegated, epistemic authority should remain proportional to demonstrated defensibility, while gaps in human comprehension should be compensated by transparent provenance, independent verification, reproducibility, adversarial scrutiny and explicit qualification. Version 0.2 adds a constructive corollary: epistemic contribution and epistemic authority should be separable. A person may originate a valuable idea without being qualified to validate it; validation by others should add warrant without erasing the originator's contribution. This motivates <strong>epistemic handoff</strong>, the structured transfer of candidate knowledge toward appropriate expertise while preserving contribution provenance. The broader economic implication is that as generation becomes cheap, scarcity migrates toward judgement, comprehension, verification, expert attention, uptake and responsible stewardship.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">1 · The decoupling</p>
        <h2>Good work no longer implies a commensurately capable author.</h2>
        <p>For much of scholarly history, producing a technically sophisticated artefact was itself partial evidence that its author possessed the competence required to produce it. That inference was never perfect: researchers rely on collaborators, instruments, software, statistical packages, prior literature and institutional expertise. Nevertheless, authorship and comprehension were often coupled strongly enough for the finished artefact to function as a rough signal of the author's capabilities.</p>
        <p>Generative machine cognition weakens that coupling. A person can now direct a search, request alternative formulations, select an apparently persuasive argument and publish a polished result without possessing the knowledge required to reconstruct its reasoning or recognise a subtle failure.</p>
        <p>The important asymmetry is therefore not simply human versus machine intelligence. It is the possibility that <strong>artefact sophistication can grow faster than curator comprehension</strong>.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p>The central risk is not that a machine-assisted artefact is necessarily poor. It may be excellent. The risk is that the person granting it social authority cannot reliably tell the difference.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">2 · Dunning–Kruger is background, not foundation</p>
        <h2>The problem is metacognitive calibration, not a meme.</h2>
        <p>Kruger and Dunning's 1999 experiments connected poor task performance with poor self-assessment and proposed a metacognitive mechanism: some of the skills required to perform well are also required to recognise good performance. That observation is intuitively relevant to machine-assisted authorship.</p>
        <p>But the popular "Dunning–Kruger effect" is too blunt a foundation for the present argument. Later work has shown that common demonstrations can be affected by regression to the mean and other statistical structure, and has argued that the magnitude or generality of the classic effect may be substantially smaller than often claimed.</p>
        <p>The Authorial Capacity Constraint therefore requires no strong version of Dunning–Kruger. It needs only the weaker and less controversial premise that human self-assessment is imperfect, that expertise and metacognitive calibration are not identical, and that fluent machine output can make the boundary of one's own understanding harder to perceive.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">3 · The Authorial Capacity Constraint</p>
        <h2>Production capacity and stewardship capacity can diverge.</h2>
        <p><strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint:</strong> the epistemic reliability of machine-amplified authorship is constrained by the human steward's capacity to understand, interrogate, verify and appropriately qualify the claims being advanced, unless those functions are supplied through other transparent and reliable mechanisms.</p>
        <p>This constraint does not require the human to reproduce every operation personally. Modern knowledge has always depended on distributed cognition. A physicist need not fabricate a detector; a biologist need not inspect the source of every statistical library; an engineer need not personally derive every theorem used by a compiler.</p>
        <p>The crucial distinction is between <em>delegation with warranted reliance</em> and <em>delegation without awareness of the reliability boundary</em>. The first is ordinary epistemic interdependence. The second can produce authority without adequate grounds.</p>
        <p>Machine cognition greatly increases the scale at which the second condition can occur because it can generate plausible reasoning at very low marginal cost and in domains remote from the curator's established expertise.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">4 · Principle of Defensible Stewardship</p>
        <h2>Cognition may be delegated. Responsibility cannot be.</h2>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Proposed principle</p>
          <blockquote>Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility.</blockquote>
          <p>As machine cognition expands the sophistication of intellectual artefacts beyond the unaided capacity of their human curators, trustworthy knowledge production increasingly depends on demonstrable comprehension, transparent provenance, verification, reproducibility, adversarial scrutiny and explicit disclosure of the curator's epistemic limits.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The principle is deliberately asymmetric. It does not say that people should publish only what they can derive unaided. Such a rule would discard the benefits of distributed and machine-augmented cognition. Instead, it says that the <em>authority claimed for an artefact</em> should reflect the quality of the support beneath it.</p>
        <p>A curator who cannot personally defend a specialised proof may still responsibly publish it if independent experts verify it, the derivation is reproducible, the provenance is available and the curator accurately represents their own role. A curator who cannot distinguish a correct proof from a polished error should not imply equivalent personal mastery merely because the artefact carries their name.</p>
        <p><strong>The right to publish need not require mastery. The right to claim mastery should.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">5 · Separate the roles</p>
        <h2>“Author” is becoming an overloaded word.</h2>
        <p>Machine-assisted knowledge production can involve several distinct contributions that traditional authorship compresses into one label. A more transparent system should be able to distinguish them:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Initiator:</strong> first introduces the idea, anomaly, question or direction.</li>
          <li><strong>Problem framer:</strong> turns the initial disturbance into a tractable question.</li>
          <li><strong>Search director:</strong> chooses prompts, branches, constraints and stopping conditions.</li>
          <li><strong>Generator:</strong> produces candidate arguments, code, models or representations.</li>
          <li><strong>Selector:</strong> decides which candidates merit further attention.</li>
          <li><strong>Developer:</strong> formalises, extends or strengthens a promising candidate.</li>
          <li><strong>Domain steward:</strong> brings relevant expertise and accepts responsibility for the interpretation of claims in that domain.</li>
          <li><strong>Verifier:</strong> tests correctness, robustness, provenance and reproducibility.</li>
          <li><strong>Interpreter:</strong> connects the result to context, consequences and limitations.</li>
          <li><strong>Realizer:</strong> turns sufficiently warranted knowledge into an intervention, system, institution or product.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A single person may occupy several roles. A machine may contribute substantially to generation, search and criticism. External experts or automated systems may contribute to verification. What matters is that the public record does not quietly convert contribution in one role into an unsupported claim of competence in all the others.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">6 · Corollary of Separable Epistemic Roles</p>
        <h2>Contribution should survive the handoff to authority.</h2>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Proposed corollary</p>
          <blockquote>Origin deserves credit. Authority requires warrant.</blockquote>
          <p>The ability to originate a valuable idea does not imply the authority to validate it, and the authority to validate an idea does not erase the contribution of its originator.</p>
        </div>
        <p>This matters because machine cognition can help a non-expert notice, articulate or search toward something valuable before they possess the expertise required to know whether it is valuable. A system that demands expert authority at the moment of initiation will throw away some of the gains of cognitive abundance. A system that grants authority merely because someone initiated an idea will manufacture false expertise.</p>
        <p>The constructive response is <strong>epistemic handoff</strong>: preserve the contribution and its provenance, state the authority gap explicitly, then route the candidate object toward the missing epistemic functions.</p>
        <p>Such a system requires at least two distinct records: <strong>contribution provenance</strong>, describing who initiated, framed, developed, selected or realised the work; and <strong>epistemic authority</strong>, describing who can defend which claims and on what evidence, expertise, replication or formal verification that authority rests.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Low authority should trigger handoff, not erasure.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p>This principle also protects the initiator from appropriation. Expert validation can strengthen an idea without rewriting who contributed it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">7 · An epistemic-status protocol</p>
        <h2>Lower barriers to production. Raise transparency of status.</h2>
        <p>Agalmic Research will experiment with explicit epistemic-status labels rather than treating publication as a binary transition from "unpublished" to "authoritative". The labels are not a ladder and do not substitute for evidence; they disclose different forms of support.</p>
        <div class="status-grid">
          <div><strong>Exploratory</strong><span>Proposed for criticism; substantial claims may remain weakly defended.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Curator-defended</strong><span>The human curator has undergone adversarial questioning and can defend the central argument.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Externally reviewed</strong><span>Relevant independent expertise has critically examined the work.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Empirically supported</strong><span>Important claims have survived specified observations or experiments.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Reproduced</strong><span>Independent reproduction or replay has confirmed a material result.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>Machine assistance should also be disclosed where it materially shapes reasoning, structure, analysis or prose. The protocol proposed here asks not merely <em>whether AI was used</em>, but <em>what epistemic work the human is actually able to stand behind</em>, and now also <em>which contributions should remain credited even when authority is supplied elsewhere</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">8 · Economics of the constraint</p>
        <h2>Cognitive abundance displaces scarcity into stewardship and uptake.</h2>
        <p>The Authorial Capacity Constraint is an instance of the broader Agalmic Research thesis that abundance in one productive factor does not abolish scarcity. It changes where scarcity binds.</p>
        <p>When candidate prose, hypotheses, code and analysis become inexpensive to generate, the scarce complements increasingly include attention, judgement, domain understanding, verification, trust, institutional legitimacy and the capacity to realise good ideas in the world.</p>
        <p>A further scarcity follows from separable epistemic roles. If many more people can originate plausible candidate knowledge, society requires enough capacity to <strong>notice, route, evaluate, validate, integrate and develop</strong> those contributions. Agalmic Research calls this the <strong>Epistemic Uptake Constraint</strong>.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>As candidate knowledge becomes abundant, the capacity to absorb and steward it becomes scarce.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <p>Expertise therefore does not become worthless under cognitive abundance. Some forms may become more valuable because the volume and sophistication of candidate output increase faster than society's ability to validate it. Expert attention itself becomes an allocative resource.</p>
        <p>This creates possible market failures. Machine assistance can cheaply manufacture the signals traditionally associated with expertise, while scarce evaluative attention can be captured by credentials, wealth, prestige or proprietary ranking systems. The challenge is not merely producing more knowledge. It is building institutions capable of receiving it without allowing the filters to become the new monopolies.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">9 · Research programme</p>
        <h2>The principles should become testable.</h2>
        <p>This version deliberately stops short of claiming validated metrics for defensibility, contribution or uptake. Several research questions follow:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>How should comprehension and defensibility be operationalised without reducing expertise to trivia recall?</li>
          <li>Does machine assistance systematically increase the gap between perceived and demonstrated understanding?</li>
          <li>Which forms of adversarial questioning best distinguish recognition, memorisation and genuine transfer?</li>
          <li>When can independent verification compensate for limited curator comprehension, and when is direct expertise indispensable?</li>
          <li>Can provenance systems record the division of epistemic labour without becoming bureaucratic theatre?</li>
          <li>How should initiation, development, validation and realization credit be represented when those roles belong to different people?</li>
          <li>How can promising low-authority ideas be routed toward expert attention without credential capture, idea appropriation or flooding?</li>
          <li>How do audiences update trust when epistemic-status and contribution disclosures are explicit?</li>
          <li>Which disciplines require different thresholds because errors carry different costs?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>A useful empirical programme would compare unaided authors, machine-assisted authors and domain experts on artefact quality, self-assessed understanding and performance under novel adversarial questions. A complementary institutional programme would test whether explicit handoff and provenance mechanisms improve the rate at which promising contributions from non-experts are identified, fairly credited and successfully validated.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 · Deep future</p>
        <h2>When epistemic authority leaves the human scale.</h2>
        <p>The Authorial Capacity Constraint may describe a transitional problem rather than a permanent equilibrium. Today it is unusual, and often uncomfortable, for a human curator to publish work they cannot fully defend. If machine cognition becomes substantially more capable, that condition could become ordinary at the frontier of knowledge.</p>
        <p>Ray Kurzweil's account of the technological singularity imagines intelligence increasingly escaping ordinary biological limits and becoming predominantly non-biological. The present paper does not depend on Kurzweil's forecasts. It extracts a narrower epistemic question from that possibility: <strong>what happens to the authority of human understanding if the production of reliable knowledge itself advances beyond human-scale comprehension?</strong></p>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Speculative conjecture · Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon</p>
          <blockquote>There may be domains in which the frontier of reliable knowledge advances beyond the point at which any unaided human can personally comprehend and defend the best available results.</blockquote>
          <p>If so, epistemic authority would no longer be grounded primarily in a person's capacity to possess the relevant reasoning. It would migrate toward systems of formal verification, replicated computation, institutional checks, machine-to-machine criticism and other structures whose reliability humans can audit only partially.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Mathematics may be an early test case. Formal proof already permits a distinction between a proof being mechanically checkable and a proof being illuminating to a human mathematician. Recent machine systems have demonstrated increasingly capable automated reasoning on olympiad-level problems, and formal proof assistants make correctness amenable to machine verification. These developments do not establish the stronger conjecture that frontier mathematics will become humanly incomprehensible. They make the separation between <em>verified truth</em> and <em>humanly possessed understanding</em> unusually easy to imagine.</p>
        <p>If that separation widened, today's Principle of Defensible Stewardship would need to evolve. "Defensibility" could no longer mean that a human can personally reconstruct the full argument. It might instead mean that the human can defend the <em>verification architecture</em>: why the formal system should be trusted, how independent checks were performed, where assumptions enter, what kinds of failure remain possible and which conclusions the machinery actually warrants.</p>
        <p>This would be a profound inversion. For centuries, expert authority has been associated with knowing more than non-experts. Under sufficiently advanced machine cognition, the most responsible expert might instead be the person most capable of identifying <strong>what no human in the loop genuinely knows directly</strong>, while maintaining trustworthy access to results that exceed human cognitive scale.</p>
        <p class="muted">This section is deliberately speculative. It proposes a direction for philosophy of knowledge, mathematical practice and epistemic economics rather than a forecast.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">11 · Reflexive application</p>
        <h2>Agalmic Research is part of the experiment.</h2>
        <p>This programme is being developed through extensive interaction with large language models. Machine cognition contributes to exploration, synthesis, drafting, criticism, code and the discovery of adjacent arguments. The human curator chooses directions, accepts or rejects outputs, asks for criticism and decides what is published.</p>
        <p>Those activities constitute genuine intellectual labour, but they do not establish expert mastery of economics, epistemology, psychology, statistics, information theory or every other field the programme may touch.</p>
        <p>They also illustrate why contribution and authority must be separated. The human curator may deserve provenance credit for originating a question, directing the search or recognizing a promising branch even when subsequent validation requires other people with stronger domain authority. Those validators should receive credit for the warrant they add without absorbing the originator's role.</p>
        <p>Future Agalmic Research publications should therefore state both their epistemic status and, where useful, their contribution roles. Where central claims outrun the curator's present ability to defend them, the appropriate response is not concealment and not silence. It is disclosure, handoff, criticism, study, verification and revision.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Open production should make both epistemic limits and contribution provenance more visible, not easier to hide.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">12 · Limitations</p>
        <h2>What this version does not establish.</h2>
        <p>The Authorial Capacity Constraint, Principle of Separable Epistemic Roles and Epistemic Uptake Constraint are presently conceptual proposals, not empirically validated laws. No quantitative measure of artefact sophistication, comprehension, defensibility, contribution, uptake or epistemic authority is established here. The boundary between legitimate distributed cognition and irresponsible delegation is likely domain-dependent. Verification mechanisms can themselves fail, and expert consensus is neither infallible nor immune to institutional incentives.</p>
        <p>The paper also does not establish that generative AI uniquely causes overconfidence. Its stronger claim is structural: AI makes it possible to produce increasingly sophisticated artefacts without requiring a matching increase in the human curator's domain competence. The epistemic and institutional consequences of that possibility require investigation.</p>
        <p>The Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon is more speculative still. Current progress in machine reasoning does not demonstrate that knowledge will become inaccessible to human understanding, nor that mathematics must be the first domain in which this occurs.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">AI-assistance declaration</p>
        <h2>How this paper was produced.</h2>
        <p>This working paper was developed through substantive interaction with OpenAI's ChatGPT. The system contributed to conceptual exploration, terminology, structure, drafting, counterargument generation and literature discovery. The human curator initiated the underlying concern, selected the research direction, challenged and revised the framing, introduced the deep-future question of diminished human epistemic authority, and subsequently introduced the distinction between idea initiation and epistemic authority together with the need for systems that can hand promising ideas to people better able to evaluate them.</p>
        <p>Version 0.2 incorporates that latter contribution as the Principle of Separable Epistemic Roles, epistemic handoff and the Epistemic Uptake Constraint. References and factual claims should be independently checked before any peer-reviewed submission. The human curator accepts responsibility for publication while explicitly declining to imply expert-level mastery of every argument in this release.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

